  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 236 PM CDT Thu Sep 3 2026\n\n(Saturday Night - Labor Day)\n\nSubtle changes have been noted for the latter part of the Labor Day \nholiday weekend forecast. Notably, the surface high pressure across \nthe Great Lakes has trended a bit stronger on ensemble guidance, \nhelping to push the backdoor cold front more to the west. It appears \nmore likely that much of (if not all) the area should see some low-\nlevel cold air advection behind this front as winds turn out of the \neast/northeast. Now high temperatures will not exactly be \"cool\" by \nany means, but should drop on average a few degrees each day from \nSaturday through Labor Day. Highs more in the low to \nmid 90s are forecast. Dewpoint (relative humidity) values are \nalways harder to forecast, but there is a pretty strong signal \nfor a fairly significant push of dry air to advect in from the \nGreat Lakes. Probabilities for dewpoints to be below 65 degrees \nLabor Day afternoon are above 60% areawide, with along/east of \nthe Mississippi River even having pretty good chances for sub \n60 degree values (over 40%). So, not only are temperatures \ncooling, but lower humidity is also likely to move into the \nregion by Labor Day. If this holds true, we may at least get a \nbrief break from heat index values near 100 degrees across the \nentire area for Labor Day, and many locations beginning Sunday \nbehind the backdoor front.\n\n\n(Tuesday - Next Thursday)\n\nThere remains high confidence in a return to near-record breaking \ntemperatures by Wednesday as mid/upper level ridging moves back over \nthe mid-Mississippi Valley. The strength of the 500-hPa level ridge \nis likely to be record breaking for early September. In \naddition, the 850-hPa thermal ridge is also expected to bisect \nthe CWA, with temperatures at that level well above +20C (>99th \npercentile). This is also 6 days out, with mostly hot and dry \nconditions so soils should continue to dry out. In theory, that \nmay yield our hottest temperatures of the entire stretch. The \nlatest NBM has widespread upper 90s with some locations around \nthe century mark, which looks pretty representative. If \nanything, it may end up a touch warmer than currently forecast.\n\nUncertainty increases sharply Wednesday night into next Thursday due \nto the possibility of a cold frontal passage. Two of the four \nclusters of the 500-hPa height pattern from the LREF (37% of \nmembers) show the anomalous ridging holding strong while the other \ntwo clusters (63% of members) show more zonal flow with a stronger \ntrough as well crossing the Great Lakes. The former pattern would \nmean a continuation of near-record breaking heat while the later \nwould send the cold front through the area and bring much cooler \n(closer to normal) temperatures. The inter-quartile range of the NBM \nshows this spread well, with about 10-15 degrees between the \n25th/75th percentiles, and nearly 15-25 degrees from the 10th \nto the 90th percentile.",
